window.WIKI_ENTRY={"id": "concept-013", "name_zh": "期望期待", "name_en": "expectation", "tagline": "赛斯说，期望（expectation）是把你内在的想法「切换」成物质实相的主要扳机——你期望生活怎样运转，它就怎样运转；光有欲望不够，得升华为笃定的期望，而负面的期望也会像传染病一样把自己变成现实。", "explanation": "在赛斯体系里，「期望」（expectation）是把内在想法切换成物质实相的主要扳机。它和模糊的「欲望」不同：欲望可能成长为期望，但只有欲望不够，必须升华为笃定的期望，物质建构才会发生。你预期生活怎样运转，它就怎样运转——你的经验、你的身体、你的健康、你的人际关系，都跟着你的预期走。负面期望会像传染病一样把自己变成现实，正面期望（尤其是带着愉快未来的 anticipate）则是症状的解药。这个原则在死后与梦中同样成立：你相信魔鬼就会遇见魔鬼，那只是你自己的心念形相。麻烦在于，我们常把社会、性别、角色「期待我们什么」内化了，却很少问自己期待自己什么；而真正有力的是反过来——用想象力辅以伟大的期望，有意识地把期待翻译成具体形式，做自己生命风景里最好的画家。知性的期望会触发身体机制，但意识心常被期待单独作业而忽略直觉的内在资料。简言之：检查你的期望，因为它正在一字不差地创造你的实相。", "definitions": [{"zh": "如果一个人想要改变他的命运，只有欲望是不够的，期望才够。欲望可能成长为期望，但光有欲望并不够。期望其实是将内在资料转换到物质建构界域的主要触发器。没有它，就不会有物质建构。", "en": "If a man wants to change his fate, desire is not enough, but expectation is. Desire may grow into expectation, but alone it is not enough. Expectation is actually the main trigger that switches inner data into the realm of physical construction. Without it, no physical construction results.", "source": "Session 76"}, {"zh": "欲望、愿望与期待主宰所有行动，而且是一切实相的基础。", "en": "Desire, wish and expectation rule all actions, and are the basis for all realities.", "source": "Session 427"}, {"zh": "我说得非常实际，你的期望如实创造了你所知的生活，你对车子的态度需要调整，只要你坚信买不起自己想要的那种车，就会如实成真。", "en": "I am speaking in highly practical terms. Your expectations literally create your life as you know it. Your attitude toward transportation needs adjustment. As long as you believe firmly that you cannot afford the kind of automobile that you want, this will be literally true.", "source": "Session 335"}, {"zh": "预期不但在所有的感官资料背后，而且形成了感官资料。", "en": "Expectation is behind all Sense data, and forms it.", "source": "Session 300"}], "blocks": [{"id": "B01", "title": "期望到底是什么，跟「欲望」有什么不同", "note": "期望不是模糊的愿望。赛斯给了一个很硬的定义：如果一个人想改变命运，只有欲望是不够的，期望才够——欲望可能成长为期望，但单有欲望不成。期望实际上是把内在资料切换到物质建构界域的主要触发器，没有它就不会有物质建构。欲望、愿望与期待三者主宰所有行动，是一切实相的基础。期望控制一个人运用与操纵意念之能量的方式；在这个系统里，「暗示」其实是个不准确的词，用「期望」好得多，因为同一类的内在指引活动在所有行动形态里都相关。意念之所以能像电一样发动酵素、自动开始物质建构，正是顺着你内在的预期走的。", "src": "第76、427、79、163、86、202、159、177、90节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 76 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 427 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 79 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 163 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 86 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 202 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 159 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 177 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 90 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-013-e0070", "zh": "如果一个人想要改变他的命运，只有欲望是不够的，期望才够。欲望可能成长为期望，但光有欲望并不够。期望其实是将内在资料转换到物质建构界域的主要触发器。没有它，就不会有物质建构。", "en": "If a man wants to change his fate, desire is not enough, but expectation is. Desire may grow into expectation, but alone it is not enough. Expectation is actually the main trigger that switches inner data into the realm of physical construction. Without it, no physical construction results.", "source": "早期课第2册 · 第76节", "year": "1964", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第2册", "session": "76", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0271", "zh": "现在。欲望、愿望与期待主宰所有行动，而且是一切实相的基础。", "en": "Now. Desire, wish and expectation rule all actions, and are the basis for all realities.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第427节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "427", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0108", "zh": "一个注。期望控制个人利用和运作意念能量方式的途径。", "en": "Now a note concerning the way that expectation controls the manner in which an individual utilizes and manipulates the energy of idea.", "source": "早期课第2册 · 第79节", "year": "1964", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第2册", "session": "79", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0174", "zh": "因此暗示是行动的特性之一，暗示这个词用得并不好。由于它在你们系统内运作，也在人类人格之内运作，所以称它为期望会好得多。不过，期望只是一个阶段而已，因为在所有行动的形态中，同一类的内在指引活动其实互有关联。", "en": "Suggestion is therefore one of the characteristics of action. The term suggestion is a poor one. As it operates within your system, and within the human personality, the word expectation is a much better term. Nevertheless, expectation is only one phase, for the same kind of inner directive activity is pertinent within all forms of action.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第163节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "163", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0147", "zh": "这里有些极为重要的交互作用。为求简单，你可以说意念是带电的，因为它们在这个机制内发动酵素，然后自动开始物质建构的工作，这符合之前概述过的内在预期。", "en": "There are interactions here of utmost importance. For simplicity’s sake you may say that ideas are electric, in that they spark enzymes within the mechanism, that then automatically begin the work of physical construction, in line with inner expectations as outlined earlier.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第86节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "86", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0198", "zh": "暗示可以塑造未来，这是理所当然的，因为期待加进来了。暗示可以改变梦，而梦本身就可以像行动般的运作。强烈的梦是一个可以比任何物质经验更重要的心灵行动，它可以完全改变人格的方向。", "en": "Suggestion then can shape the future. Expectation enters in here particularly of course. Suggestion can shape dreams, and the dreams themselves then operate as action. A strong dream can be a more significant psychic action than any physical experience, and it can change the course of the personality completely.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第202节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "202", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0171", "zh": "我们能量的焦点极其重要，你我都一样。期望大幅决定了我们使用能量的方式，以及即将形成的建构类型。举例来说，你朋友的溃疡完全是他的问题，他建构出自己身体上的实体物质。", "en": "Our focus of energy is vital, both yours and mine. Expectation to a large extent determines the manner in which we will use this energy, and the types of constructions that will be formed. Your friend’s ulcer for example is his problem in its entirety, constructed into the physical matter of his own organism.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第159节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "159", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0186", "zh": "因此，我们已经明白醒时人格可以提供暗示给睡梦中的人格，这些指示会被执行。不过，睡梦中的人格会根据自己的了解，以自己的方式来执行。要求的解决方法，或许不会依照意识上的自己所期望的方式呈现。意识上的自己甚至可能还搞不清楚那个解决方案已经到手，即使如此，这个解决方案还是可以执行。", "en": "We have seen therefore that suggestions may be given by the waking personality to the sleeping personality, and these directions will be followed. They will be followed however by the sleeping self in its own fashion, and according to its own understanding. The solutions asked for may not appear to the conscious self in the fashion that it expects. The conscious self may not even recognize that it has been given a particular solution, and yet it may act upon the solution.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第177节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "177", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0149", "zh": "接下来这个听起来可能有点盲目乐观（Pollyannaish），但他应该要特别努力去帮助别的人类，再小的忙都可以，而不期待别人的感谢，一周三次。我不建议，你知道，他故意每天做这件事，免得它在心理上变成一个自我牺牲的仪式。我也很强烈建议，一周三次，以一种非常安静、自律但正向的方式，每当有事发生而他抱持的看法有别于已经提出的一个看法时，他要特别努力去表达他自己。", "en": "This next may sound Pollyannaish to an extreme, but he should make it a point to help another human being in any small way, without expecting thanks, three times a week. I do not suggest, you see, that he do this on purpose daily, lest it develop psychologically into a self-sacrificial ritual. And I also most strongly suggest that three times a week in a very quiet, disciplined but positive manner, he makes it a point to express himself when any matters arise where he holds a diverse opinion from the one being presented.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第90节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "90", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B02", "title": "你活出的，正是你预期的", "note": "这是整组概念最核心的一句话：你预期你的实相会以何种方式运转，它就会照那种方式运转。预期不只在所有感官资料背后，它「形成」了感官资料。你的期望如实创造了你所知的生活——只要你坚信自己买不起想要的车，那就真的买不起。经验是你自己的心智、精神、有意识与无意识的思想感受共同的产品，而这组产品里就织进了你的期望；你以为外来的、强加的环境，其实是你自己的想法、期待与渴望生出来的。你是一个把自己的期待翻译成具体形式的创造者，外在的显现不过是你内在渴望的翻版。你创造梦境也顺着你的预期走，自我也是按照内在而非外在的预期在创造物质。天堂地狱都只是表象，你根据信念和预期创造实相，所以值得把你的信念和预期看个清楚。", "src": "第157、300、335、609、614、115、119、329节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 157 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 300 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 335 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 609 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 614 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 115 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 119 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 329 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-013-e0169", "zh": "关于你的预期本质，说再多都不够，因为你预期你的实相会以何种方式运转，它就会根据那种方式运转。", "en": "Enough cannot be said along these lines regarding the nature of your expectations, for according to the manner in which you expect your reality to behave, in that manner shall it be.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第157节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "157", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0228", "zh": "你仍然必须和物质实相打交道，因为它绝对是内在资料的重现。我不停地说这件事：预期不但在所有的感官资料背后，而且形成了感官资料。我跟你说过——顺便说一下，我可爱的怀疑论者，以前我告诉过你——你创造你自己的物质环境和宇宙。你的物质状况在每一方面都反映着你内在的预期、成就以及失败，而且我也告诉过你，任何疾病都是一个内在扭曲造成的结果。", "en": "You must still deal with physical reality, for it is definitely the representation of inner data. I have said this constantly: Expectation is behind all Sense data, and forms it. I have told you—and incidentally my lovely skeptics I have told you in the past—that you create your own physical environment and universe. Your physical condition in every way is a reflection of your inner expectations, accomplishments, and failures, and I have told you that any illness is the result of an inner distortion.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第300节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "300", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0236", "zh": "我说得非常实际，你的期望如实创造了你所知的生活，你对车子的态度需要调整，只要你坚信买不起自己想要的那种车，就会如实成真。", "en": "I am speaking in highly practical terms. Your expectations literally create your life as you know it. Your attitude toward transportation needs adjustment. As long as you believe firmly that you cannot afford the kind of automobile that you want, this will be literally true.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第335节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "335", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0312", "zh": "经验与体会是“心智”、“精神”、“有意识的思想与感受”，以及“无意识的思想与感受”的产品。这几样东西加起来就形成你个人所知的实相。因此，你并不会受到外来的、不可抗拒的或是强加于你的外界环境所左右。由于你太过将自己与组成你生活经验的那些具体事件连接在一起，使得你往往很难分辨仿佛是发生在外界的事件，和令它们发生的那些思想、期待或渴望。", "en": "Experience is the product of the mind, the spirit, conscious thoughts and feelings, and unconscious thoughts and feelings. These together form the reality that you know. You are hardly at the mercy of a reality, therefore, that exists apart from yourself, or is thrust upon you. You are so intimately connected with the physical events composing your life experience that often you cannot distinguish between the seemingly material occurrences and the thoughts, expectations and desires that gave them birth.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第609节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "609", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0316", "zh": "我不是一个有形体的“人”，但是，基本上，你们也不是。只不过你们现在的感受及体验很“具体”。你是一个将自己的期待带到具体形式里的“创造者”。这个世界的意义就在于做你自己的“参考点”，而所有外在的显现其实也就是你自己内在渴望的翻版。你可以改变自己个人的天地，而实际上你一直也都在这样做，只不过是行焉而不察罢了。你真正要做的，只在于如何去有意识地运用自己本有的能力，去细察自己的想法与感受的性质，然后把那些你基本上赞同的想法、感受投射出来而已。", "en": "I am not a physical personality. Basically, however, neither are you. Your experience now is physical. You are a creator translating your expectations into physical form. The world is meant to serve as a reference point. The exterior appearance is a replica of inner desire. You can change your personal world. You do change it without knowing it. You have only to use your ability consciously, to examine the nature of your thoughts and feelings and project those with which you basically agree.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第609节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "609", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0320", "zh": "你的经验像一块布，而这块布是你透过自己的信念与期盼织出来的。你心目中对自己以及对实相本质抱持的观念，都影响着你的思想与情绪。你把自己对实相抱持的信念当作一项真理，几乎连问都不问，因为每件事情看起来都这么的顺理成章。对你而言，这些事情本身就是一种事实的“声明”，明显的连审视一下都是多余的。", "en": "You form the fabric of your experience through your own beliefs and expectations. These personal ideas about yourself and the nature of reality will affect your thoughts and emotions. You take your beliefs about reality as truth, and often do not question them. They seem self-explanatory. They appear in your mind as statements of fact, far too obvious for examination.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第614节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "614", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0156", "zh": "我现在准备回答了。第一个问题我认为指的是就一般认定的成长而言的成长，一种就身体和心智接近期望中的实现而言的一种日渐成熟。", "en": "I will also, though not necessarily at this session, but in this session or the next session, have some remarks concerning the man who asks for the answers.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第110节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "110", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0159", "zh": "任何个人经验到的梦的类型，或梦的种种类型，由很多因素决定。我现在说的是发生在当下的梦经验，而不是他的自我允许他在意识上记得它的残迹。就像一个个人依据他的能力和缺点创造他的物质形象和环境，而且与他的预期以及潜意识和内在需求一致那样，他也创造他的梦；而且这些与他已经创造的外在环境互相作用。", "en": "The type of dream, or the types of dreams experienced by any individual, is determined by many factors. I am speaking now of the dream experience as it occurs, and not of the remnant of it that his ego allows him to consciously recall. As an individual creates his physical image and environment according to his abilities and defects, and in line with his expectations and subconscious and inner needs, so does he create his dreams; and these interact with the outer environment which he has created.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第115节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "115", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0233", "zh": "这个幻觉的持续时间很短。其实，天堂和地狱只不过是表象。它们代表最初的直觉洞见。但是没有那些说法所称的天堂或地狱存在。在宇宙之内或在任何宇宙或系统之内，没有它们的位置。你根据你的信念和预测创造实相。因此，你应该非常清楚地检视你的信念和预期。", "en": "This will last very briefly. Heaven and hell, indeed, are mere representations. They represented originally intuitive insights. But no heaven or hell exist in those terms. There is no place within the universe or within any universe or system for them. You create reality according to your beliefs and expectations. Therefore, it behooves you to examine your beliefs and expectations very clearly.", "source": "早期课第7册 · 第329节", "year": "1968", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第7册", "session": "329", "only_en": true}]}]}, {"id": "B03", "title": "负面期望会伤你，正面期望能救你", "note": "期望有方向性，方向错了就很危险。负面的预期根本保护不了个人或他接触的人，反而会变成像任何传染病一样或大或小的破坏力。反过来，高度的期望会提升暖气系统的效能，低度的期望会降低它——你害怕去信任，才是最大的因素。对财务的重大改善来说，你的态度本身筑起了一道屏障，把本可自动发生的情况挡在门外，因为你的预期效益把改善拒之门外。但若你预期一个愉快的未来，这种努力是对所有症状都极好的解药，再怎么强调也不为过。当你把眼前的困难连同它想象的结果投射到未来，你就把期望调转来对付自己，反而招来你最想避免的情况。你对自己期望越多，你得到的也越多；而那些内在心理的实相，会替你的期望染上颜色。", "src": "第143、79、345、220、335、222、76、10、422节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 143 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 79 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 345 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 220 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 335 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 222 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 76 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 10 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 422 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-013-e0164", "zh": "但是，你应尽的责任，也是每一个人在他的力量范围内应尽的责任，就是维持他自己的心灵健康与生命力；依据这股生命力的强度，他将会保护他自己和别人。负面预期，根本无法保护个人或那些他接触到的人，实际上反而会和任何传染病一样，变成或大或小的破坏力。", "en": "However it is your duty, and the duty of every individual insofar as it is within his power, to maintain his own psychic health and vitality; according to the strength of this vitality he will protect himself and others. Negative expectations, far from protecting either the individual or those with whom he comes in contact, will actually, to a greater or lesser degree, turn as destructive as any epidemic.", "source": "早期课第3册 · 第143节", "year": "1964–65", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第3册", "session": "143", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0121", "zh": "至于现有的暖气系统，高度的期望将会增加它的效益。低度的期望则会减低它的效益。你们害怕去信任才是最重要的因素。你们两个很坚定地怀疑你们在物质宇宙的实际作用为何，而且你们会一直怀疑，以你们目前的心态，你们不会去测试你们的效用如何。就算你们敢，你们的恐惧也会打败你们，这只会更加让你们相信自己的无用。", "en": "With the present heating system, high expectations would have increased its efficiency. Low expectations would have decreased its efficiency. Your fear of commitment was indeed an element here. You have both steadily doubted your practical efficiency in a material universe, and you will continue to do so, for with your present attitudes you will not test your efficiency. Or if you dare test it, your fears will defeat you, and only serve to convince you further of this inefficiency.", "source": "早期课第2册 · 第79节", "year": "1964", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第2册", "session": "79", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0245", "zh": "这样的改善很大一部分是基于一个事实，就是鲁柏在私人课的冥想中，再次期待愉快的未来。（在ESP课里）这样的努力可以克服所有的症状，这一点再怎么强调也不为过。（停顿）", "en": "A large percentage of this improvement is based upon the fact that Ruburt is anticipating a future joyfully again, in the contemplation of classes, privately given. (In ESP.) .This sort of endeavor cannot be stressed too highly as an antidote to all symptoms. (Pause.)", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第345节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "345", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0203", "zh": "现在。当你以这样的方式反应，你不只聚焦在现在的困难，而且还投射了这种困难和其想像的结果到未来，让你所想要的落空，而因此带来了你恰巧希望避免的那种情况。", "en": "Now. When you react in such a manner, not only do you focus upon the present difficulty, but you project the difficulty and its imagined consequences into the future, turning expectation against you, and therefore bringing about the very conditions which you wish to avoid.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第220节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "220", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0239", "zh": "当然，你在其他方面已经跟以往大不相同。你的态度显然形成一种障碍，不利于财务上的各种重大改善。一些财务状况本来可以自动改善，但由于你的预期效益，就将这样的改善拒之于门外。 不用说也知道，全职工作会改善财务状况，但我指的不是这种事情。如果——非常大的如果——如果你改变了预期，而创造出一种源源不绝的心灵气候，那么在改善经济状况的同时，基本上也可以保留跟目前一样让你乐在其中的工作时间表。那些你想都没想过的门就会打开。", "en": "In other respects of course there have been profound changes. By your attitude you are effectively forming a barrier against any financial betterment of any significant kind. You repulse the circumstances in which such betterment could take place automatically, because of the effect of your expectations. It goes without saying that a fulltime job would better the financial situation, but this is not the sort of thing to which I am referring. Your situation could be bettered financially while you retain basically the same schedule you now enjoy, if—and a very large if—if changed expectations were creating the psychic climate in which supply would flow. Doors would open that you do not know existed.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第335节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "335", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0205", "zh": "整体来说，你们做得很好，比你们所认为的要好很多。过去你们会当作是自然而接受的反应，现在会知道那反应的本身是不利的。但这样的认知有时候会让你们认为你们没有进步，但事实上是有的。现在，你期待自己越多，你就会得到越多。", "en": "You are doing very well, much better than you think, generally speaking. Reactions which you would have accepted as natural in the past, you now accept as being unfavorable, as they are. But the recognition, you see, causes you at times to think you are not making progress when indeed you are. You expect more of yourself now, and you are getting more.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第222节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "222", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0083", "zh": "你要对一位72岁的老太太做什么不关我的事。请原谅我的幽默，因为这是个很严肃的话题。我忍不住。但是，所有这些内在心理实相扭曲了你的期望，就像鲁柏完全相信一个货真价实的作家生不出小孩一样。", "en": "Now, what you would want to do with a seventy two year old woman is beyond me. Forgive my humor, since the subject is serious. I couldn’t resist. But, all of these inner psychological realities color your expectations, just as Ruburt is entirely convinced that a writer of real merit cannot bear children.", "source": "早期课第2册 · 第76节", "year": "1964", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第2册", "session": "76", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0426", "zh": "胎儿是被对未来的生长与发展的期待所推进的。去预期最不幸的情况会恶化，而非改进，已经够糟了，但相信人类必然会毁灭它自己，或核子毁灭几乎是不可避免的，真的就是鲁莽了。", "en": "The fetus is propelled by the expectation of future growth and development. It is bad enough to anticipate that most unfortunate situations will worsen rather than improve, but it is foolhardy indeed to believe that mankind is bound to destroy itself, or that nuclear destruction is nearly inevitable.", "source": "健康之道 · 第10节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "10", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0269", "zh": "我们还有一项注。鲁柏自发的天性是宽容的、明朗的，按他自己的标准来说是相当天生的善良——当他否认自发性而试图制造这些特质时，便觉得它们如此困难。我现在期待一个明确的好转，而鲁柏一准备好，我们就会开始我的书。", "en": "We have one further note. Ruburt’s spontaneous nature is tolerant, sunny, quite natively good in his terms—qualities that he finds so difficult when he tries to manufacture them while denying spontaneity. I expect now a definite change for the better, and as soon as Ruburt is ready, then we will begin my book.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第422节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "422", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B04", "title": "期望与身体、健康、疾病", "note": "身体并不是 passively 被心智支配的。身体意识本身是独立的，它自己的防卫机制在很大程度上保护它不受心智负面信念的侵害；在许多人从所谓生病状态不知不觉回到健康状态的例子里，身体意识是完全不受负面预期或观念阻碍地运作的。每个原子、细胞、器官里都住着「生长与繁荣」的期望，所有生命的部分都含着这份乐观的期待，并被允诺它们的能力会成熟。但你对健康的期望大大影响细胞的存在；当你真的「期待」自己会病倒，那可以当成不去面对许多问题的借口，社会随口给的暗示就足以让潜伏的病毒被激活、体温真的升高。你无法靠研究别人「期待你什么」来认识自己，只能问自己期待自己什么。过去事件不会自己闯进来，除非被你心中有意识的期待和思想召唤；若你嘴里说「我充满平静」却期待好结果，那只会盖住感受、压抑能量。", "src": "第637、1、2、814、5、632、663、2、1节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 637 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 1 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 2 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 814 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 5 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 632 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 663 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-013-e0332", "zh": "在你自己之内，你拥有“意识”创造性参与的所有潜能。细胞不必要有意识地觉知到你，才能完成它自己，虽然你对健康的期望大大地影响了它的存在，但你对灵魂和存在的认知，能助你将这些其他次元的能量导入你的日常生活中。", "en": "You possess within yourself all of those potentials in which consciousness creatively takes part. The cell does not need to be consciously aware of you in order to fulfill itself, even though your expectations of health largely influence its existence, but your recognition of the soul and entity can help you direct energies from these other dimensions into your daily life.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第637节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "637", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0413", "zh": "身体意识的确是独立的。到一个很大的程度，其本身的防卫机制保护它不受心智之负面信念侵害——至少到一个很大的范围——如我先前提到过的，几乎所有的人都通过一个所谓的生病状态回到健康状态，而对于这改变毫不知觉。在那些例子里，身体意识运作得不被负面的预期或观念所阻碍。", "en": "The body consciousness is indeed independent. To a large degree its own defense mechanisms protect it from the mind’s negative beliefs — at least to a large extent. As I have mentioned before, almost all persons pass from a so-called disease state back into healthy states without ever being aware of the alterations. In those cases the body consciousness operates unimpeded by negative expectations or concepts.", "source": "健康之道 · 第1节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "1", "only_en": true}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0418", "zh": "这生长和繁荣的期望是住在每个原子、细胞和器官内的，而所有生命的部分都包含这乐观的期待，并且被赐予它们的能力将长到成熟的允诺。", "en": "This expectation to grow and flourish is addressed within each atom, cell, and organ, and all of life’s parts contain this optimistic expectation and are blessed with the promise that their abilities will grow to maturity.", "source": "健康之道 · 第2节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "2", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0383", "zh": "你真的期待会病倒，它可以作为不去面对许多问题的借口。有许多人几乎有意识的觉察他们在做什么，所有他们需要做的只是对社会如此张扬的提出的暗示付出注意而已，体温真的上升了，关切使得喉咙变干。潜伏的病毒——到现在为止还没造成任何伤害——真的被激活了。", "en": "You are literally expected to come down with the flu. It can serve as an excuse for not facing many kinds of problems. Many people are almost consciously aware of what they are doing. All they have to do is pay attention to the suggestions offered so freely by the society. The temperature does rise. Concern causes the throat to become dry. Dormant viruses — which up to now have done no harm — are activated.", "source": "个人与群体事件的本质 · 第814节", "year": "1977–79", "tag": null, "short": "个人与群体事件的本质", "session": "814", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0423", "zh": "这种人的确表演了一种人际关系的艺术，做出，好比说，交响的、情感性的曲子，那的确熟练地弹奏感情，就像钢琴家弹奏琴键一样。借由看看你自己的生活、跟随你自己的冲动与倾向，你可以相当容易地发现自己的能力在哪个区域。你无法借由研究别人期待你什么，学到关于自己的事——却只能借由问自己，你期待自己什么，而替自己发现你的能力在哪个方向。", "en": "Instead, such people will indeed perform a kind of artistry of relationships, composing, say, symphonic, emotional compositions that indeed play as masterfully upon the emotions as the pianist upon the keys. By looking at your own life, you can quite easily discover in what areas your own abilities lie by following the shape of your own impulses and inclinations. You cannot learn about yourself by studying what is expected of you by others — but only by asking yourself what you expect of yourself, and discovering for yourself in what direction your abilities lie.", "source": "健康之道 · 第5节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "5", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0328", "zh": "你有意识地发出要反应的信号，而非其反面。过去的事件，除非它们被你们心中有意识的期待和思想召来，否则不会像这样入侵。（停顿。）那些无意识的记忆将按照你目前的信念而被发动。当你的思想发动了愉悦的身体感觉和实质事件，你将得到补充和更新；或当你把不愉快的过去肉体的遭遇带到你的觉知里来时，你会感到沮丧。", "en": "You consciously give the signals for reaction. It is not the other way around. Past events do not intrude in this manner unless they are beckoned by the conscious expectations and thoughts that exist within your mind. (Pause.) Those unconscious memories will be activated according to your current beliefs. You will be replenished and renewed as your thoughts motivate joyful body sensations and physical events, or you will be depressed as you bring into your awareness unpleasant past body happenings.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第632节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "632", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0340", "zh": "每一个人必须由你们自己的实相之点来做。没有其他的办法。如果你觉得充满了愤怒，那么，不要说：“我充满了平静。”而期待好的结果发生，如此做的话，只会掩盖了你的感觉，而抑制了你的能量与力量。如果你非常生气——那就打一个枕头而体验那个怒气，但不要对他人施暴。把怒气释放出来直到你筋疲力尽，如果你老实地这样做，你将明白暴怒的理由，那理由常是相当明显的，你只不过不想面对它们罢了。", "en": "Each of you must work from the point of your own reality. There is no other way. Period. If you feel filled with rage, then do not say, “I am filled with peace,” and expect results. You will only be blanketing your feelings and inhibiting your energy and power. If you are furious, then beat a pillow and experience the rage, but without violence to another. Work it through until you are physically exhausted. If you do this honestly the reasons for the fury will come to you, and they will often be quite obvious. You simply did not want to face them.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第663节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "663", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0420", "zh": "（我说我一直在等她的腿开始动，以致她能动得更多，而离开床坐进一个椅子之类的东西里，但，既然那还没发生，我们也没什么可做的。在这之前我期待腿的动作，但我们必须筝持。", "en": "(I said I’ve been waiting for her legs to begin to move, so that she can move more, and get out of the bed into a chair of some sort, but since that hasn’t happened yet there isn’t much we can do. I’d expected leg movement before this, but we’ll have to wait.", "source": "健康之道 · 第2节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "2", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0415", "zh": "（我们抹上了口红，以她细致的皮肤和缺乏大多数同龄的人会有的皱纹，她看来非常好。她五十四岁。我告诉她，她看来出奇的好。她的头发看来也不错——卷曲而活泼。我说如果它染过，如她一向做的，看来会很好，跟她的老样子一样。我也建议她每天至少短短地照照镜子，很快地便完全没什么难了。她也许甚至会期待看见自己继续的进步呢。", "en": "(We put on lipstick and she looked very good, with her fine skin and lack of wrinkles that most people her age have. She’s 54. I told her she looked remarkably well. Her hair also looks good — curling and alive. I said if it was dyed, as she used to do, that she’d look fine, just like her old self. I also suggested she look in the mirror, at least briefly each day, and that soon there would be nothing to it. She might even get to look forward to seeing herself continue to improve.", "source": "健康之道 · 第1节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "1", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B05", "title": "死后与梦中，经验也跟着期望走", "note": "期望塑造实相的原则，不只在清醒的物质生活里成立，在死后经验、梦经验、任何出体遭遇里也一样。你的经验跟着你的期望走：如果你执迷于恶的概念，就会碰上恶的情况；你若相信魔鬼，就会遇见它，还不知道那只是你自己的心念形相。对死后生命有信心的人，会更容易适应新情况；没有这种信仰的人，只要持之以恒、有所期待、有决心，也能靠书里的练习把知觉扩展到这些层面。你们期待物质世界五花八门的元素彼此契合形成秩序，同样的「彼此契合」也包括了主观生活——你私人的梦其实是更庞大的梦实相里的片段。转世的人格很清楚自己「等待中的」受孕何时发生，会被不可抵抗地牵引到那个时空点。人在梦中带着 anticipation，而一个人若每次死亡都期待完全的灭绝、随后却发现存在仍在继续，便会有一种「神圣喜剧」的感觉。坚持要外在感官证据才肯信，就像期待照相机播放音乐一样荒谬。", "src": "第538、535、935、557、200、518、25、170节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 538 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 535 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 935 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 557 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 200 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 518 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 25 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 170 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-013-e0295", "zh": "换言之，你的经验跟随着你的期望走。在死后与梦中的经验以及任何“出体”的遭遇中，这个原则也适用。如果你执迷于恶的概念，那你就会碰上恶的情况。如果你相信魔鬼，那你就会碰见它。如我先前提过的，当意识不是针对着肉体时，它有更大的自由。再说一次，思想和感情不经过时间流逝就建构实相。因此如果你相信你会遇上一个恶魔，你就会创造出这样的一个“心念形相”来，却不知它乃是你自己的创造物。", "en": "Your experience, in other words, follows your expectations. Now the same applies to after-death experience and to the dream experience, and to any out-of-body encounters. If you are obsessed with the idea of evil, then you will meet evil conditions. If you believe in devils, then you will encounter these. As I mentioned earlier, there is greater freedom when consciousness is not physically directed. Thoughts and emotions are constructed, again, into reality without the physical time lapse. So if you believe you will be met by a demon, you will create your own thought-form of one, not realizing that it is of your own creation.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第538节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "538", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0292", "zh": "那些对死后生命有信心的人，将发现你自己更容易适应新的情况。至于那些没有这种信仰的人，也许可由一个不同的方式获得它，也就是照我在此书后面会给你们的练习去做。如果你是不懈怠的、有所期待的、有决心的。这些练习将使你能扩展你的知觉到实相的这些其他层面去。", "en": "Those of you who had faith in life after death will find it much easier to accustom yourself to the new conditions. Those of you who do not have such faith may gain it in a different way, by following through in the exercises I will give you later in this book; for these will enable you to extend your perceptions to these other layers of reality if you are persistent, expectant, and determined.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第535节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "535", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0404", "zh": "那么，你们一定会有许多较大的梦构造（formations），那只能被称为集体梦（group dreams）——你们自己的梦在其中发生，并参与其中的主观事件。你们期待物质世界所有的素质，不论多不同，都彼此契合而形成某种的永久性及秩序。那么，这同类的“彼此契合”也包括了主观的生活——或比如说，你私下的梦也是一个更大的梦实相里的片段，应该不会令你惊奇。私人梦对那个实相之运作的重要性，就如电子对你们物质实相一样的重要（停顿良久），为智慧及乐趣的累积提供了内在的途径。", "en": "(Long pause at 8:50.) You are bound to have, then, many larger dream formations that can only be called group dreams—subjective events in which your own dreams happen, and in which your own dreams take part. You expect all of the elements of the physical world, however diverse, to fit together and form a certain kind of permanency and order. It should be no surprise, then, that this same kind of “fitting together” includes subjective life also—or that, say, your private dreams are also fragments in a vaster dream reality. They are as important to the operation of that reality as electrons are to your physical one (long pause), providing inner pathways for the accumulation of wisdom and pleasure.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第935节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "935", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0310", "zh": "就如同我告诉过你们的，不管事情是怎么样发生，决定都是老早就做好了的，所以要转世的人都知道“他期待中的胎儿”何时会形成，不管他是否打算在一受孕时就入胎，他在人母受孕的那一刹那就一定会“被牵引”至当时的现场。", "en": "Now: In all cases the decisions have been made ahead of time, as I told you. The reincarnating personality is aware, therefore, when the conception for which it has been waiting takes place. And while it may or may not choose to enter at that point, it is drawn irresistibly to that time and point in space and flesh.", "source": "时空之外 · 第557节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "时空之外", "session": "557", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0196", "zh": "关于鲁柏的第二个梦，首先，许多有待探索的房间和公寓，代表了不同面向的心灵实相，因此他在梦中是期待着的。在公寓里的商店与奠基在过去世经验的物质面向和不同的物质有关。", "en": "Now, concerning Ruburt’s second dream. Let me say first of all that the many rooms and apartments to be explored all represented various facets of psychic realities, hence his anticipation in the dream. The shop within the apartment had to do with the physical dimension and various physical abilities which are based on past-life experiences.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第200节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "200", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0288", "zh": "当一个人生生死死了许多次，每死一次都期待着完全的灭绝，当他随之发现，存在仍然在继续的时候，那么就有了一种“神圣的喜剧”的感觉。（译注：此处套用但丁《神曲》原名，语意双关而幽默。）", "en": "When one has been born and has died many times, expecting extinction with each death, and when this experience is followed by the realization that existence still continues, then a sense of the divine comedy enters in.", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第518节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "518", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0008", "zh": "同样，内在感官接收到的印象，其实是以一种你现在还不了解的方式具体呈现。这个资料对头脑也有实质的效应。从外界刺激获得的印象以同样的方式影响着头脑，它们也在脑部上制造印象。就像任何经验改变一个人格一样，它们也会改变人格。坚持要有外在感官资料的证据，和期待照相机播放音乐一样是个荒谬的想法。", "en": "Again, the impressions received by the inner senses are actually concrete in a way that you do not yet understand. This data also has physical effects upon the brain. In the same manner that impressions received from outside stimuli affect the brain, they make their impression upon it. They change the personality as any experience changes a personality. To insist upon evidence in terms of outside sensual data is as ridiculous a notion as to expect a camera to play music.", "source": "早期课第1册 · 第25节", "year": "1963–64", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第1册", "session": "25", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0179", "zh": "事实上，长久以来我一直在期待能有一些情境，让我们现在可以善加利用，以帮助我们自己。不过，我现在最关注的并不是向你证明我自己存在的事实。（语气强烈）我们就撕开天窗说亮话，你关注于证明你的存在给我看吗？几乎不会，你理所当然认为我可以觉察到你的存在，而我也要跟你确认我真的可以觉察到你，但我也不关注于证明我的存在给你看。然而，我对教育非常感兴趣，如果我必须掏出法宝耍些花招来传达我的观念，那我就会这么做。", "en": "I myself have indeed long awaited some circumstances which we now can take advantage of for our own benefits. However, I am not mainly concerned with proving for you the fact of my own existence. （Strong voice.） Let us now be honest. Are you concerned with proving your existence to me? Hardly. You take it for granted that I am aware of your existence, and I assure you so I am. But neither am I concerned with proving my existence to you. I am however very interested in education. If I have to pull a few tricks out of the bag to get my ideas across, then so I shall.", "source": "早期课第4册 · 第170节", "year": "1965", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第4册", "session": "170", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B06", "title": "社会、性别与角色里的期望", "note": "很多痛苦来自我们把别人或社会「期待」的东西内化了。在一个表面传统、底下难搞的家庭里，教条要求母亲生养完美的孩子、至少在表面上屈从于男性。最细微的偏离都被惊慌看待，于是个人的身份与价值完全系在「够不够男／够不够女」上——每个类别都被期待要有完全不同的特质、能力与表现。你不可能先把男孩教成「坚强沉默型」，又期待他在语言或社会关系上出类拔萃；你也不可能教导女孩「女人是直觉的、直觉反逻辑、必须不惜代价女性化」，又期待她展现出强而逻辑的思维。被教导去害怕自己天性中「男性面」的女人，也没法被期待去爱男人。狂热份子无法容忍，他们期待服从。许多读者期待像鲁柏那样有通灵能力的人就该完全健康、富有、有智慧、没有任何人的情感——这种投射本身就是错位的。就连科学教条也期待人不加证明、只凭信心就相信它。人们旅行各国时不期待它们一模一样，却要求未知实相的「快照」必须彼此一致，否则就慌张地修饰。", "src": "第679、768、771、774、854、10、666、14、714节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 679 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 768 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 771 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 774 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 854 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 10 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 666 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 14 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 714 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-013-e0353", "zh": "在表面上，这家庭是非常传统的，但在其下却是极难处理的。在这家中存在着一些教条，比如说，这母亲被期待养出完美的孩子，而且她，至少表面上，应屈从于男人。", "en": "It was on the surface a very conventional structure, yet underneath, highly unwieldy. There were dogmas. The mother was expected to bear perfect children and to be subservient to the male, at least in outward fashion.", "source": "未知的实相卷一 · 第679节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷一", "session": "679", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0371", "zh": "最细微的偏离也被惊慌的看待，因此个人的身份与价值，完全维系于和女性或男性身份认同上。大家期待每种性别的人有完全不同的特征、能力和表现。因此，一个不感觉他自己是十足男性的男人，就不信任他作为一个人的身份。一个怀疑她自己不是完全的女性的女人，也同样不信任她人性的完整。", "en": "The slightest deviation is looked upon with dismay, so that personal identity and worth are completely tied into identification with femaleness or maleness. Completely different characteristics, abilities, and performances are expected from those in each category. A male who does not feel himself fully male, therefore, does not trust his identity as a person. A woman doubtful of her complete femininity in the same manner does not trust the integrity of her personhood.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第768节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "768", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0374", "zh": "许多读者可以明显看出，这是个习得的行为。你也不能教一个男孩成为 「坚强缄默型的男人」，而后又期待他在语言或社会关系上出人投地。你不能期待一个女孩显示「强烈的逻辑思维发展」，当她被教导一个女人是直觉的——而直觉是反逻辑的，而她必须不顾一切代价的女性化，或非逻辑，这是相当明显的。", "en": "It should be obvious to many of my readers that this is learned behavior. You cannot teach a boy to be “the strong silent male type,” and then expect him to excel either verbally or in social relationships. You cannot expect a girl to show “strong, logical thought development” when she is taught that a woman is intuitional — that the intuitions are opposed to logic, and that she must be feminine, or nonlogical, at all costs. This is fairly obvious.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第771节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "771", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0376", "zh": "被教导去害怕她们天性中「男性的」的女人，也不能期待她们去爱男人，而同类的行为产生了。", "en": "Women taught to be frightened of the “masculine” sides of their nature cannot be expected to love men, either, and the same kind of behavior results.", "source": "心灵的本质 · 第774节", "year": "1975–77", "tag": null, "short": "心灵的本质", "session": "774", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0388", "zh": "狂热份子无法忍受容忍，他们期待服从。一个民主社会为个人及这个族类提供了最大的挑战与成就的可能性，因为它容许了概念的自由交流。可是，它对其人民要求得多得多，因为大体来说，每个人必须由形形色色的生活方式与信念里挑选他（她）自己每日生活与行动的舞台。", "en": "Fanatics cannot stand tolerance. They expect obedience. A democratic society offers the greatest challenges and possibilities of achievement for the individual and the species, for it allows for the free intercourse of ideas. It demands much more of its people, however, for in a large manner each must pick and choose from amid a variety of life-styles and beliefs his and her own platform for daily life and action.", "source": "个人与群体事件的本质 · 第854节", "year": "1977–79", "tag": null, "short": "个人与群体事件的本质", "session": "854", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0428", "zh": "许多男人期待有儿子，却同时尊敬婚姻为可敬的家庭生活的一个必要部分，而同时觉得婚姻不知怎地贬低了人——尤其是男人——而性行为本身只在它带给他一个子嗣时才是正当的。", "en": "Many men look forward to having sons, while at the same time they revere marriage as a necessary part of respectable family life, and also feel that marriage is somehow degrading — particularly to a male — and that the sex act itself is only justified if it brings him an heir.", "source": "健康之道 · 第10节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "10", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0344", "zh": "能量的集中是追随着你的信念，而许多本身并非负面的信念，经过强调之后，会导致那些确实看起来是负面的结果。 对那些发现自己在不喜欢的情况里的许多人，这资料是极为重要的。为了几个理由，我会以鲁柏来做例子。你们许多人都有一个倾向，相信任何像鲁柏这种能力的人，就应该没有问题或挑战了。鲁柏常常说：“我有些读者期待我完全的健康、富有且聪明，而真的不再有任何属于人类的感情。”他讲得很对。", "en": "This material is highly important to many of you who find yourselves in situations with which you are not pleased. I am using Ruburt as an example for several reasons. Many of you have a tendency to believe that anyone with such abilities as Ruburt has, has no problems or challenges. Ruburt has often said, “Some of my correspondents expect me to be completely healthy, wealthy and wise, and indeed beyond any human feelings,” and he is quite correct.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第666节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "666", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0431", "zh": "你无法将哲学自生活分离，因为你的思维和意见给你的生命其意义与原动力。有些人相信，生命是无意义、没有目的的，而其无数的部分是光靠机率落到一起的。显然，在此我说的是科学的教义，但这种教义是远较宗教性而没那么科学的，因为它也期待只靠信心而没有证据地被相信。", "en": "You cannot divorce philosophy from life, for your thoughts and opinions give your life its meaning and impetus. There are some people who believe that life is meaningless, that it has no purpose, and that its multitudinous parts fell together through the workings of chance alone. Obviously I am speaking here of scientific dogma, but such dogma is far more religious than scientific, for it also expects to be believed without proof, on faith alone.", "source": "健康之道 · 第14节", "year": "1984", "tag": null, "short": "健康之道", "session": "14", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0361", "zh": "不过，如果在那些心灵的快照里有任何不相符之处，人们就担心起来。虽然你们期待你们自己实相的照片各有不同，可是那些旅行到未知的实相里的人，如果他们的快照不彼此一致的话，他们就变得担忧起来，因此，他们就拼命试着使所有的照片看起来相像。换言之，他们加以修饰。", "en": "If there were discrepancies among the snapshots, however, people worried. While you expect pictures of your own reality to be diverse, those who journeyed into the unknown reality became concerned if their snapshots did not agree, so they tried desperately to make all of the pictures look alike. They touched them up, in other words.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第714节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "714", "only_en": false}]}]}, {"id": "B07", "title": "有意识地运用期望：你就是自己的画家", "note": "既然期望这么有力，你就该反过来有意识地去用。你是一个创造者，把自己的期待翻译成具体形式；世界只是你的参考点，你可以改变自己的个人天地，而且其实一直都在改，只是没察觉。真正的动力在敢于臆想尚未实现之事的想象力里——想象力辅以伟大的期望，能带来可能性范围之内几乎任何的实相，所有1980年的可能版本都会发生，只是你选定的那一个会进入意识经验。做你自己最好的画家吧：你的思想、情感与期望就像活生生的笔触，你在生命风景里画出自己的角落，它们和别人的思想混合，提供实质事件从中形成的广大精神成分。问题在于，意识心常被期待要单独作业、忽略那些本可为它所用的高度直觉性内在资料。你旅行各国不期待它们相同，出体旅行也不会每次都到同一地点。想象两栋屋子，一栋代表负面建构、一栋代表你所期望的建构，心里要清楚具体指哪些观念。那些欲望与期待的力量曾强烈到逼使一切万有去寻找实现它们的方法。而期望的确能创造具体的改善——就像珍全神贯注在车子会修好上，一小时后它真的好了。", "src": "第891、873、621、710、481、427、240节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 891 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 873 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 621 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 710 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 481 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 427 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 240 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-013-e0398", "zh": "真正的动力存在于敢臆想尚未实现之事的想象力里（热切的）。想象力，辅以伟大的期望，可带来在可能性范围之内的几乎任何的实相。1980年的所有可能版本都将发生。除了那些你们选定的，所有其它的将留在心理的外围，在你们有意识的经验的背后——但所有这些可能版本将在某方向相连。", "en": "The true power is in the imagination which dares to speculate upon that which is not yet (intently). The imagination, backed by great expectations, can bring about almost any reality within the range of probabilities. All of the possible versions of 1980 will happen. Except for those you settle upon, all of the others will remain psychologically peripheral, in the background of your conscious experience—but all of those possible versions will be connected in one way or another.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第891节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "891", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0396", "zh": "今晚的主题：“伟大的期望”（Great Expectations。译注：中译为《块肉余生录》，为狄更斯著名小说）——因为我在此提到狄更斯的书。", "en": "(With humor:) Tonight’s subject matter: “Great Expectations”—for I am here referring to the book by (Charles) Dickens.", "source": "梦、进化与价值完成 · 第891节", "year": "1979–82", "tag": null, "short": "梦、进化与价值完成", "session": "891", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0393", "zh": "以你们的说法，不论在任何时期，每个活着的人都帮忙画出了当代文明之活生生的画面。做你自己最好的画家。你的思想，情感与期望就像是活生生的笔触，你在生命的风景中用以画出你自己的角落。如果你在自己的生活里全力以赴，那么，你就的确帮忙改进了所有生命的品质。你的思想就与雪花或雨滴或云彩一样的真实，它们与其他人的思想混在一起，以形成人类活生生的风景，提供了实质事件自其中形成的广大精神成分。", "en": "Each person alive helps paint the living picture of civilization as it exists at any given time, in your terms. Be your own best artist. Your thoughts, feelings and expectations are like the living brush strokes with which you paint your corner of life’s landscape. If you do your best in your own life, then you are indeed helping to improve the quality of all life. Your thoughts are as real as snowflakes or raindrops or clouds. They mix and merge with the thoughts of others, to form man’s livingscape, providing the vast mental elements from which physical events will be formed.", "source": "个人与群体事件的本质 · 第873节", "year": "1977–79", "tag": null, "short": "个人与群体事件的本质", "session": "873", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0324", "zh": "因此之故，这些人期待意识心能独立作业，可以这样说，忽略掉那些本可为它所用的极为直觉性的内在资料。人们假定意识心不该觉察到这种资料。但任何一个人却又相当明白，直觉性的预感、灵感、预知或千里眼资料，常常会由心底浮现成为有意识的知识。通常它们会被推到一边不被理会，因为别人早已教你，意识心不应当赞同这种“无稽之谈”。因此，别人告诉你要信赖你的意识心，同时却又让你相信，意识心只能觉察来自外在物质世界的刺激。", "en": "The conscious mind was [therefore] expected to perform alone, so to speak, ignoring the highly intuitive inner information that is also available to it. It was not supposed to be aware of such data. Yet any individual knows quite well that intuitive hunches, inspiration, precognitive information or clairvoyant material has often risen to conscious knowledge. Usually it is shoved away and disregarded because you have been taught that the conscious mind should not hold with such “nonsense.” So you have been told to trust your conscious mind, while at the same time you were led to believe it could only be aware of stimuli that came to it from the outside physical world.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第621节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "621", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0359", "zh": "在你们的世界里，你由一个国家旅行到另一个国家，而你不会期待它们全都相像。反之，正因为它们之间的不同，你才去探访世界形形色色的部分——所以，并不是每次出体旅行都会带你到同样的地点。", "en": "In your world you travel from one country to another, and you do not expect them to be all alike. Instead, you visit various parts of the world precisely because of the differences among them — so all out-of-body-journeys do not lead to the same locale.", "source": "未知的实相卷二 · 第710节", "year": "1974–75", "tag": null, "short": "未知的实相卷二", "session": "710", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0280", "zh": "想象吹过那片土地的夏日之风，此刻正把芬芳的空气灌满屋内。让第一间屋子代表一切负面的观念或建构物，让新屋子代表你所期望的那些观念或建构物。不过，你们心里要牢牢地清楚，这些具体指的是哪些观念。", "en": "Imagine the summer winds that blow over the land that now fills the interior of the house with scented air. Let the first house represent all negative ideas or constructions , and the new house represent the desired ideas or constructions . Have it firmly in your mind however as to what ideas these refer, specifically.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第481节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "481", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0273", "zh": "所以，为了你们的缘故，我将谈到某个难以形容的遥远过去，那些事件便发生在其中。用你们的话说，这些欲望与期待的力量和活力，后来变得如此难以承受，以至于一切万有不得不去寻找使它们产生的方法。", "en": "So I will speak, for your benefit, of some indescribably distant past, in which these events occurred. The strength and vitality of these desires and expectations, in your terms then, became so insupportable that All That Is was driven to find the means to produce them.", "source": "早期课第9册 · 第427节", "year": "1969–70", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第9册", "session": "427", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0213", "zh": "（看着技师与车子搏斗，我感觉很确定他不够熟悉这件事，所以不知道自己在做什么。我表达了我的感觉之后就离开，前往画廊告诉珍。当时赛斯正开始给我们的资料谈的是预期的力量；珍坚持说我们回去取车时，车子就修好了。我们在一家小餐馆吃饭，珍全神贯注在车子修好的预期上。当我们1小时后回去时，它真的修好了。加油站的技师告诉我们，他很惊讶自己居然能够修好它。这个事件曾经让我相当沮丧，但那种沮丧现在并不会困扰我。", "en": "（Watching the mechanic struggle with the car, I felt sure he was not familiar enough with the work to know what he was doing. I made my feelings known, then left for the gallery to tell Jane. This was at a time when Seth was beginning to Jill us in on the power of expectation; Jane insisted that the car would be ready when we went back for it. We ate in a diner, and Jane concentrated on the car being ready. When we went back for it an hour later it was indeed ready. The station mechanic told us he was surprised that he had been able to fix it. The incident had upset me considerably, in a way that would not bother me now.", "source": "早期课第6册 · 第240节", "year": "1967", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第6册", "session": "240", "only_en": true}]}]}, {"id": "B08", "title": "期望、信念与心智的协作", "note": "知性（intellect）把族类自然的目的与意图翻译成「合适的」文化语境，而知性的期望与意图会自发自动地触发适当的身体机制，带来必要的环境互动，透过知性表达的意图指导你对世界的经验。你不能期待一个还没准备好面对生活的人突然开窍；一个人若把难过的现在投射到未来，那几乎是他内在唯一「能期待」的东西，于是被字面地诠释出来。在私人课里，鲁柏再次期待愉快的未来，这种努力能克服所有症状。但也要现实：他不能期待自己的课完全没问题，却可以期待在一段持续时间里整体成功。当一个人期待一个强大的内我来接管整个人格，自我反而被这个想法吓坏、躲了起来——他砸开自己的门去求开悟，却还撑不住。直觉的背景够强时，能在一瞬间扫掉不幸的期望与各种投射。一个父亲关系的议题会让人总觉得必须成功否则被惩罚，于是对内心任何失败感都恐慌，并因而期待年长男性的拒绝。恨在一种奇怪的意义上是回到爱的方法，若不被干扰地表达，它要传达的是「存在」与「被期待之物」之间的一种分离。而如果你自己都不去尝试，你又怎能期待别人去做呢。", "src": "第7、70、217、365、354、403、673、362、532节", "cites": [{"n": 1, "id": null, "label": "第 7 节"}, {"n": 2, "id": null, "label": "第 70 节"}, {"n": 3, "id": null, "label": "第 217 节"}, {"n": 4, "id": null, "label": "第 365 节"}, {"n": 5, "id": null, "label": "第 354 节"}, {"n": 6, "id": null, "label": "第 403 节"}, {"n": 7, "id": null, "label": "第 673 节"}, {"n": 8, "id": null, "label": "第 362 节"}, {"n": 9, "id": null, "label": "第 532 节"}], "groups": [{"label": "", "note": null, "picks": [{"id": "q-concept-013-e0407", "zh": "那么，知性帮助你们的族类转化它自己自然的目的和意图——自然人的目的和意图——成它们「合适的」文化范畴，以致自然人所拥有的那些能力，能够对当代的文明有益。 那些目的和意图，真的是改变了世界。知性的期望和意图自动自发地激发适当的身体机制，以带来必要的环境上的互动，而透过你的知性来表达你的意图，指导你对世界的经验。", "en": "The intellect, then, helps your species translate its own natural purposes and intents — the purposes and intents of the natural person — into their “proper” cultural context, so that those abilities the natural person possesses can benefit the civilization of its time. Those purposes and intents literally change the world. The intellect’s expectations and intents spontaneously and automatically trigger the proper bodily mechanisms to bring about the necessary environmental interactions, and your intent as expressed through your intellect directs your experience of the world.", "source": "神奇之道 · 第7节", "year": "1980", "tag": null, "short": "神奇之道", "session": "7", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0044", "zh": "我们现在先把精神错乱放到一边，但这件事表现出你的一个疏忽，以及你对隐藏自己最好的一面所表现出来的一种傲慢。因此你不能期待她能了解。它不在她的特质与经验所及的范畴里，但是你可以把它带进她的经验范畴里；而那些在她经验范畴里的东西，才会成为她特质的一部分。", "en": "We will let the demented bit pass for now, but this represents a failure on your part, and a somewhat smug attitude of hiding what is best from view. You cannot therefore expect her to understand. It is not within the realm of either her nature or experience, but you can bring it within the realm of her experience; and what is within the realm of her experience becomes part of her nature.", "source": "早期课第2册 · 第70节", "year": "1964", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第2册", "session": "70", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0201", "zh": "他坚定的立场拯救了整个情况，因为你的父母很清楚地明白了什么是可以期待的，而什么是不能期待的。很明显的，没有任何方式可以证明这样的沟通曾发生过。不过在那些你梦到特定对象的夜晚，你可以强烈的怀疑那人也梦到你。困难点是那个对象很可能并没有训练自己去回忆他的梦，所以我们能证明的不多。", "en": "This firmness on his part saved the situation, for your parents clearly understood what could be expected and what could not be expected. There is obviously no way to prove that this communication took place. On those evenings however when you dream of a certain individual, you may strongly suspect that the same individual is dreaming of you. The trouble is that the other individual most likely has not trained himself to recall his dreams, and so we can prove little.", "source": "早期课第5册 · 第217节", "year": "1966", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第5册", "session": "217", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0255", "zh": "我将谈谈有关佛德瑞克·菲尔的一些资料。还记得我说过，你们跟他之间的事会很顺利。（微笑，赛斯大概在两年前给了我们这个预测）不过，后来出现了不好的期待与各种不同的揣测，但是在你们第一次见面后，由于直觉的氛围背景够强大，很快就将之一扫而空，这不是巧合。", "en": "I will go into some material concerning Frederick Fell . You remember that I told you that affairs with him would work out well. (A faint smile. Seth gave us this prediction perhaps two years ago.) Unfortunate expectations and various projections operated here however, but the intuitive background was enough to wipe these out within an instant of your first meeting. This was no coincidence.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第365节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "365", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0249", "zh": "看了我们的朋友鲁柏，觉得很有趣，他今天真的把我的话正确听进去了。他不能指望自己的课完全没问题，我几乎没答应过这点，不过，持续一段时间之后，他可以期望在课堂安排上整体而言会非常成功，所有主要的面向都大放异彩。他必须为成功而努力，但这种成功是他个人天生已配备好，只要努力就可获致。", "en": "It was amusing to watch our friend Ruburt. He did correctly hear me today. He cannot expect no problems with his classes; I hardly promised that. He can expect an overall successful class arrangement, however, over a sustained period, successful in all main aspects. He will have to work for success, but this is the kind of success he is personally equipped to work for and achieve.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第354节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "354", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0262", "zh": "同样的，这也反映在你开车的方式上。好，你与父亲之间的关系所造成的问题，也为你带来了其他方面好的影响。举例来说，这种某程度上与你当个成功的老师有关，因为这让你有了权威，如果可以的话，你会像你驱车驾驶一样督促学生，迫使他们以每分钟85英里的速度行驶。不过，你催促他们比催促自己还容易，你成为一位出色的老师。但是，在你内心深处，你总是在说——看吧，老爸，我做得非常好——因为你心目中的这个老爸，总是在你的肩膀后面看着你、评断你。好，我现在描述的就是你的态度。你觉得自己必须成功，否则他会惩罚你，你觉得自己必须十全十美；因此，你对于自己内心的任何失败感都会恐慌，并且夸大自己的失败，所以你今晚来到我这里，仿佛你才两三岁一样。如果鲁柏（珍）跳起来抓一把尺敲你的手指，你根本不会感到惊讶。现在更进一步，你因为这个原因而期望男人拒绝，这种状况只适用在年纪比你大的男人身上，你对年轻男性感到十分愉悦和满意。请稍候。", "en": "Again this is reflected in the way you drive your car. Now, the difficulties arising from your relationship with your father also gave you other beneficial effects. This feeling is somewhat responsible for your success as a teacher, for example. For you are then in authority, and you would, if you could, drive your students as you drive your car and force them to go 85 miles a minute. You are easier on them than you are on yourself, however, and you make an excellent teacher. In the back of your mind, however, you are always saying—see Daddy, I am doing something well—for this father of yours in your mind is always behind your shoulder watching you and judging you; now this is your attitude that I am describing. You feel that you must be successful or he will punish you, that you must be perfect; therefore you become panic stricken at any sense of failure within you, and you over exaggerate your failings so that you came here tonight to me as if you were two and a half years old. You would not have been at all surprised had Ruburt (Jane) jumped up grabbed a ruler and banged your fingers. Now, a step further, therefore, is that you expect rejection on the part of the male for this reason. Now this only applies to men who are older than you. You are perfectly happy and content with younger males. Give me a moment here.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第403节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "403", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0348", "zh": "那么，以一种很奇怪的方式，恨是一个回到爱的方法；未受干扰地表达了的话，恨的作用是要传达存在与所期待之间的一个分离。", "en": "In a strange manner, then, hatred is a means of returning to love; and left alone and expressed it is meant to communicate a separation that exists in relation to what is expected.", "source": "个人实相的本质 · 第673节", "year": "1972–73", "tag": null, "short": "个人实相的本质", "session": "673", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0252", "zh": "他期望有一个强大的内我来接管整个人格的控制，不过自我却被这种想法吓坏了。在他希望自我归来的不久以后，自我就隐藏了，他正在寻求开悟，但还没有强壮到可以控制它的进步。", "en": "He intended a strong inner self to take over control of the entire personality. The ego however was terrified at the thought. Long after he wished the ego to return, the ego hid. He battered down his own doors. He is seeking enlightenment, but he was not strong enough to contain it.", "source": "早期课第8册 · 第362节", "year": "1969", "tag": null, "short": "早期课第8册", "session": "362", "only_en": false}, {"id": "q-concept-013-e0290", "zh": "你可以休息一下。（突然较大声地：）如果你自己都不去尝试，你怎能期望别人去做呢？", "en": "You may take a break. (Suddenly louder): And if you do not try this, how can you expect others to?", "source": "灵魂永生 · 第532节", "year": "1970–71", "tag": null, "short": "灵魂永生", "session": "532", "only_en": false}]}]}], "reading": [], "books": [{"name": "个人实相的本质", "count": 9}, {"name": "健康之道", "count": 8}, {"name": "早期课第8册", "count": 7}, {"name": "早期课第2册", "count": 5}, {"name": "早期课第4册", "count": 5}, {"name": "早期课第3册", "count": 5}, {"name": "早期课第5册", "count": 5}, {"name": "早期课第9册", "count": 4}, {"name": "灵魂永生", "count": 4}, {"name": "个人与群体事件的本质", "count": 3}, {"name": "梦、进化与价值完成", "count": 3}, {"name": "心灵的本质", "count": 3}, {"name": "早期课第7册", "count": 2}, {"name": "未知的实相卷二", "count": 2}, {"name": "时空之外", "count": 1}, {"name": "早期课第1册", "count": 1}, {"name": "未知的实相卷一", "count": 1}, {"name": "早期课第6册", "count": 1}, {"name": "神奇之道", "count": 1}], "span": [1963, 1984], "stats": {"blocks": 8, "quotes": 70, "reading": 0, "reading_tagged": 0, "tagged": 0, "only_en": 11}};
